{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "שמע יעקב", :translit "shma ya'akov", :gloss "Jacob heard"} {:he "יחילו כשמע צור", :translit "yachilu kishma tzur", :gloss "they tremble when they hear a rock"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["שמע יעקב" "יחילו כשמע צור"]}], :frame {:text "The second occurrence cited is from Deuteronomy 32:27, where Moses recounts the song of Ha'azinu: 'they tremble (יחילו) when they hear (כשמע) a rock (צור),' referring to the nations' dread upon hearing God's acts. Ba'al HaTurim links the phrase 'when he heard' about Laban hearing of Jacob to a future redemption of Jacob's descendants.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 32:27"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ויחבק לו וינשק לו", :translit "vayechabek lo veyinashak lo", :gloss "and he embraced him and kissed him"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Midrashic reading: Laban's embrace and kiss were searches for hidden wealth, not gestures of affection."}], :frame {:text "Rashi on Genesis 29:12 cites the Midrash that Laban reasoned: Eliezer, a mere servant, came with ten camels loaded with wealth; how much more so should Jacob, who is a member of the family, bring riches. Laban embraced Jacob to feel for coins concealed in his garment, and kissed him to check for jewels hidden in his mouth. The word וינשק is written with dots over certain letters in the masoretic text, signaling that the kiss was not sincere (Genesis Rabbah 70:13; Rashi on Genesis 29:12 citing Midrash).", :sources ["Genesis Rabbah 70:13" "Rashi on Genesis 29:12"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ויחבק לו וינשק לו", :translit "vayechabek lo veyinashak lo", :gloss "and he embraced him and kissed him"} {:he "חיבקו לגזול מה שעליו", :translit "chibeko legazol ma she'alav", :gloss "he embraced him to rob what is on him"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "ויחבק לו וינשק לו", :b "חיבקו לגזול מה שעליו"}], :frame nil}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["שמע יעקב" "יחילו כשמע צור"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ש